RiSH, the RISC Shell, 0.03 (11 Nov 2026)
========================================
A Linux terminal for the RISC OS desktop, on RISC OS on Linux.

Double-click !RiSH for a terminal: a Linux shell as RISC OS's own Linux
user, the same one *IXRun uses. It has no password and can't change the
system. Type exit (or close the window) to finish.

The icon bar icon: Select opens another terminal; Adjust, or "Root shell"
on its menu, opens a root terminal, which asks for Linux's root password
first (the one *Linux and Updates use; the first time, you choose it).
A root terminal is marked so that it can't be mistaken for an ordinary
one: a red title bar that begins "ROOT:", a red cursor, and a line in
bold at the top saying that every command runs with full privileges.

Full-screen programs work: nano, vi, less, top, and anything else written
for an xterm. Install what you need from a root terminal, for example

    apt-get install nano

The window can be resized (the program inside is told) and the scroll bar
shows what has scrolled off the top (1000 lines). "Large text" on the
menu doubles the size of the text, for 4K screens; it is on to begin with
on a screen 2560 pixels wide or more.

Keys: everything goes to Linux except F12 and the other keys RISC OS
keeps. The cursor keys, Home, Copy (End), Page Up and Down, Insert,
Delete, Tab and F1 to F11 are what an xterm sends. Ctrl-C, Ctrl-D, Ctrl-Z
and the rest of the Ctrl keys work as on Linux.

Copy and paste: drag with Select to select text (Adjust moves the end of
the selection; dragging past the top or bottom scrolls). "Copy" on the
menu, or Ctrl-Shift-C, puts it on RISC OS's clipboard for any program to
paste. "Paste" on the menu, Ctrl-Shift-V or Shift-Insert types the
clipboard's text into the terminal. (Ctrl-C and Ctrl-V alone go to
Linux, as they must.)

Not yet: the mouse inside programs, italic and blinking text. Characters
RISC OS's system font doesn't have show as "?".

Up to six terminals can be open at once.

